  <div class="attributed-text-segment-list__container relative mt-1 mb-1.5 babybear:mt-0 babybear:mb-0.5">
    <p class="attributed-text-segment-list__content text-color-text !text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap break-words
        " dir="ltr" data-test-id="main-feed-activity-card__commentary">What role do relationships and bargaining power play in determining risk? According to <a class="link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Ffeed%2Fhashtag%2Fesma&amp;trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>#ESMA</a>’s latest working paper, more than you might expect. 
🔎 The research finds that borrowing conditions in EU repo markets are not determined solely by the quality of collateral exchanged. They are also influenced by factors such as a borrower’s bargaining power, competition between lenders, the diversification of its funding sources, and the strength of relationships between counterparties.

🔑 Why does this matter? As attention increasingly turns to vulnerabilities outside the banking sector, understanding how these factors influence borrowing conditions can provide valuable insights into leverage, market resilience, and financial stability.

📘 Read the full paper to learn more about the factors shaping borrowing conditions and bilateral haircuts in EU repo markets and their implications for <a class="link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/signup/cold-join?session_redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Ffeed%2Fhashtag%2Ffinancial&amp;trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>#financial</a> stability → <a class="link" href="https://lnkd.in/dQuERsYx?trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" rel="nofollow" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>https://lnkd.in/dQuERsYx</a></p>
<!---->  </div>



        

        
            
      
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    
    

